---
// The CanJam SoCal handout page. Rendered at BOTH /canjam and /card so scans off the printed
// measurement report can be told apart from anything else; the two routes are otherwise identical
// and this component is the single source for both.
//
// WRITTEN FOR: someone who just met Jamey, is holding a phone in a ballroom on bad hotel wifi, and
// will give this thirty seconds. Everything here is above the fold on a phone or close to it. No
// images, no scripts, no web fonts beyond what the shared chrome already loads.
//
// PORTFOLIO MODE. No pricing, no sales language, no call to action beyond "download" and "email
// me". If you are tempted to add a third, don't.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// EVERY FACT ON THIS PAGE WAS CHECKED AGAINST THE REPO ON 2026-08-16. If you edit it, re-check.
// The things that were wrong in the first draft of the brief are called out at each site below.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

import Nav from './Nav.astro';
import Footer from './Footer.astro';
import Button from './Button.astro';

// ============================================================================================
// The store link and the iOS status MOVED to src/data/attune.js, which is now the one place any
// surface reads them from: this handout, the landing page and the support page all share it, so
// they cannot drift apart again. The verification notes that used to sit here live there too.
//
// When iOS is approved: set IOS_STATUS in that file to 'live'. That is the whole edit, site-wide.
// ============================================================================================
import { APP_STORE, ATTUNE } from '../data/attune.js';

const IOS_LINE = ATTUNE.platformLine;
---

<Nav />

<main>
  {/* NOTE ON COMMENT STYLE IN THIS FILE: use {/​* *​/}, never <!-- -->. Astro emits HTML comments
      into the shipped page, and this one is handed to engineers and competitors who will view
      source. The reasoning below is internal. Expression comments are stripped at build. */}
  <div class="wrap sheet">
    <header class="who">
      <span class="eyebrow">CanJam SoCal &middot; August 29 and 30</span>
      <h1>Jamey Warren</h1>
      {/* Verified against index.html, which is the canonical bio: "1997-2001 GRACE DESIGN,
          EMPLOYEE #1", "2003-2017 HEADROOM, VP OPS -> CEO", "twenty-five years in audio".
          One line, as asked. No resume. */}
      <p class="lede">
        Twenty-five years in audio. Employee&nbsp;#1 at Grace&nbsp;Design, then HeadRoom as VP&nbsp;Ops and
        later President and CEO. Now measuring headphones and building the software on my own bench in
        Livingston, Montana.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="blk">
      <h2>Attune</h2>
      <p>
        Pick your headphone, then let a blind, loudness-matched A/B on your own music find the tuning you
        actually prefer. You can also voice one headphone toward another and hear the difference. It runs
        on the same correction engine as Level.
      </p>
      <p class="status">{IOS_LINE}</p>
      <Button href={APP_STORE} variant="primary" size="lg">Attune on the {ATTUNE.storeName}</Button>
    </section>

    {/* THE REASON THE PAGE EXISTS.

        HISTORY, because the reasoning still matters: the original brief asked this section to claim
        every measurement carries "a repeatability band and an error budget rather than a single
        line." That was declined, because on 2026-08-05 the only numbers available (0.37 / 3.87 dB)
        were the HeadRoom HMS II.3 ARCHIVE's seating SD, and drawing them over our curves would have
        asserted another rig's repeatability as our own.

        UPDATED 2026-08-24, after the bench day. The number is now ours and measured, and the answer
        is better than a band: reseat repeatability is a property of the HEADPHONE, not the rig.
        0.059 dB (HD 800 R) to 0.316 dB (PM-3 R) in the control band, a 5x range on one bench. So we
        still draw no band, but for the opposite reason -- not "we haven't earned a number" but "one
        number would misstate every curve it covers." Numbers: warren-labs/measurements/RIG.md.

        The differentiator is unchanged and is now evidence rather than restraint. Still the right
        pitch for this audience: every engineer reading it has seen a spec sheet quote somebody
        else's uncertainty. */}
    <section class="blk">
      <h2>How the measurements are made</h2>
      <p>
        A miniDSP EARS&nbsp;Pro with per-unit serial-matched calibration, using IEC&nbsp;60318-4 couplers, the
        coupler standard the industry rigs use.
      </p>
      <ul class="method">
        <li>
          <b>Seated for best fit, deliberately.</b> The headphone is adjusted for seal and sound the way
          someone settling in to listen would adjust it, not placed at random.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>One real seating, never a blend.</b> The published curve is the <i>medoid</i> of the run,
          between 10 and 24 reseats depending on the headphone: an actual achievable placement the data
          elected, not a computed middle. Averaging reseats publishes a bass shelf nobody will ever hear,
          and on one headphone here it published a treble peak no seating ever produced.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Raw and full band.</b> Not compensated to any target, normalized to 0&nbsp;dB at 1&nbsp;kHz. Both
          channels are measured and stored separately.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Curves from two rigs never share an axis.</b> The offset between this bench and the old
          HeadRoom fixture has not been measured, so a cross-rig difference cannot be quoted and is not
          drawn.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Repeatability is measured, and it is a property of the headphone.</b> The instrument
          contributes 0.002&nbsp;dB. Reseating moves the midrange by 0.06&nbsp;dB on an HD&nbsp;800 and
          0.32&nbsp;dB on a PM-3, so there is no single variance band to draw over every curve and we
          do not draw one. An EARS&nbsp;Pro is a house reference, not an industry reference coupler.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Two of the six are older and say so.</b> Four rows are regenerated from verified,
          archived runs. The SR60x and RS1x predate that method and have no archived run behind them.
          They are labelled, not quietly mixed in.
        </li>
      </ul>
      {/* Deliberately no corpus count in the prose: it is six headphones today (data/measurements.js,
          and attune commit f086a50 "say six headphones when six are listed"), it grows, and a number
          in copy goes stale silently. "Small and honest about it" is the standing position. */}
      <p class="small">
        The published set is small and says so. Every curve carries the rig that produced it, the operator,
        and its stated limits.
      </p>

      {/* ======================= RIG PHOTO GOES HERE =======================
          Jamey is supplying it. Drop the file at assets/canjam/rig.jpg (repo root
          assets/, not site-src/public/ — see assets/attune/README.md) and swap the
          two lines below.

          Keep loading="lazy" and the explicit width/height: this page is read on
          hotel wifi, and an undimensioned image shifts the layout under the
          reader's thumb as it arrives. Set width/height to the real pixel size.

          <figure class="rig">
            <img src="/assets/canjam/rig.jpg"
                 alt="The miniDSP EARS Pro bench with a headphone seated on it"
                 width="1600" height="1200" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <figcaption>The bench, Livingston, Montana.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          =================================================================== */}
    </section>

    <section class="blk contact">
      <h2>Get in touch</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="mailto:jamey@warrenlabs.com" class="mail">jamey@warrenlabs.com</a>
      </p>
      <p class="small">Straight to me. No form.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</main>

<Footer />

<style>
  /* Mobile-first and deliberately plain. Everything here is a shared token; nothing invents a new
     visual language. The page has no images, no scripts and no fonts of its own, so on a bad
     connection it paints as soon as the shared chrome does. */
  /* Vertical padding ONLY. A `padding:` shorthand here beats the global `.wrap { padding: 0 24px }`
     on specificity and zeroes the side gutters, which put the body copy hard against the edge of a
     phone screen. This page is read almost entirely on phones, so that is the one thing not to get
     wrong. */
  .sheet {
    padding-top: 30px;
    padding-bottom: 8px;
    max-width: 46rem;
  }
  .who {
    padding-bottom: 26px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  h1 {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: clamp(30px, 8vw, 42px);
    line-height: 1.05;
    margin: 12px 0 0;
  }
  .lede {
    margin: 14px 0 0;
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .blk {
    padding: 28px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .blk:last-child {
    border-bottom: none;
  }
  h2 {
    font-size: 20px;
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }
  .blk p {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    font-size: 15.5px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  /* The Attune platform line. Its own colour because it is the one line most likely to be edited
     on the morning of the show, and it should be findable at a glance in the rendered page too. */
  .status {
    font-family: var(--mono);
    /* Mono is wide. At 12px it fits a 320px phone without the line breaking mid-phrase; the
       min-width:600px block takes it back up. */
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--accent-soft);
  }
  .method {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0 0 16px;
  }
  .method li {
    padding: 12px 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line-soft);
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .method li:first-child {
    border-top: none;
  }
  .method b {
    color: var(--text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .small {
    font-size: 13.5px;
    color: var(--muted-2);
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  /* Big enough to hit with a thumb, and it reads as an address rather than a button so nobody
     mistakes it for a contact form. */
  /* inline-block with vertical padding so the tap target clears ~44px. As a bare inline link it
     measured 23px, which is a miss-prone target for a thumb, and this is the one link on the page
     that has to work first time. It still reads as an address rather than a button, so nobody
     mistakes it for a contact form. */
  .mail {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: clamp(15px, 4.4vw, 19px);
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--accent);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--accent-line);
    padding: 10px 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  .contact p {
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .rig img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .rig {
    margin: 20px 0 0;
  }
  .rig figcaption {
    margin-top: 9px;
    font-size: 12.5px;
    color: var(--muted-2);
  }
  .blk :global(.btn) {
    width: 100%;
    text-align: center;
  }
  @media (min-width: 600px) {
    .sheet {
      padding-top: 44px;
    }
    .status {
      font-size: 13.5px;
    }
    .blk :global(.btn) {
      width: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
